
.TH BASENAME 1 "October 2026" "basename" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
basename \- strip directory and suffix from filenames
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B basename
.I NAME
.RI [ SUFFIX ]
.PP
.B basename
.RB [ \-az ]
.RB [ \-s
.IR SUFFIX ]
.IR NAME ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B basename
outputs the final component of each specified pathname,
removing any leading directory components.
.PP
Trailing slashes are removed before processing.
If a pathname contains no slash, it is printed unchanged.
If the pathname consists entirely of slashes, the result is
.BR / .
.PP
An optional suffix may be specified and is removed from
the end of the resulting filename if it matches.
The suffix is not removed if it equals the entire filename.
.PP
By default, a single pathname is processed.
Multiple pathnames are supported with
.B \-a
or
.BR \-s .
Each result is printed on a separate line unless
.B \-z
is specified.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-\-multiple
Support multiple pathnames.
.TP
.BR \-s " " \fISUFFIX\fR ", " \-\-suffix=\fISUFFIX\fR
Remove a trailing suffix from each filename.
This option implies
.BR \-a .
.TP
.BR \-z ", " \-\-zero
End each output with a NUL byte instead of a newline.
This allows safe processing of filenames containing
newline characters.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Display usage information and exit.
.SH EXAMPLES
Extract the final component of a pathname:
.PP
.RS
.B basename /usr/local/bin/gcc
.RE
.PP
Output:
.PP
.RS
.B gcc
.RE
.PP
Remove a suffix:
.PP
.RS
.B basename /home/user/file.txt .txt
.RE
.PP
Output:
.PP
.RS
.B file
.RE
.PP
Remove a suffix using an option:
.PP
.RS
.B basename -s .txt /home/user/file.txt
.RE
.PP
Process multiple pathnames:
.PP
.RS
.B basename -a /usr/bin/gcc /usr/bin/ld
.RE
.PP
Output:
.PP
.RS
.nf
gcc
ld
.fi
.RE
.PP
Remove a suffix from multiple filenames:
.PP
.RS
.B basename -s .txt file1.txt file2.txt
.RE
.PP
Output:
.PP
.RS
.nf
file1
file2
.fi
.RE
.PP
Produce NUL-terminated output:
.PP
.RS
.B basename -az /usr/bin/gcc /usr/bin/ld
.RE
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Successful completion.
.TP
.B 1
An invalid option was specified, an invalid number of
arguments was supplied, or an output error occurred.
.SH STANDARDS
The traditional
.B basename NAME [SUFFIX]
functionality conforms to POSIX.
The
.BR \-a ,
.BR \-s ,
.BR \-z ,
and
.B \-\-help
options are extensions.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR dirname (1),
.BR realpath (1)
